#
#Citadel Messages Version 3.00
#Used with Citadel 68K Version 3.42.Q00 or later...
#
# Format:
# Col   Description
# 1-8   Message Code.  These are the codes in Citadel software.  You
#       can add your own codes, but Citadel will not use them!  You may
#       modify the text of the messages but read the text below first!
#       The CODE is made up of two parts.  The first 6 characters are the
#       identifier that Citadel uses "NOKILL" for example.  This file must
#       have a code of "NOKILLNO" for the novice or default message and
#       may also have a code of "NOKILLEX" for expert users.  Citadel will
#       always use the novice message if the expert is not found.
#
# 9     a blank, ignored, used for readability
# 10-79 Message Text.  This is the actual message text that Citadel will
#       use for the message.  You may modify this to suit your system.
#       You may expand these messages and customize them in many ways.
#       There are two types of messages.  The first is a single line of
#       text as are most of these messages in this file.  The second is
#       to tell Citadel to use a certain text file as a message.  If
#       the first character of the message text is an "@", Citadel will
#       use the filename directly following the "@" for the message text.
#
# Variables:
#       The message text lines may contain variables which are system
#       dependant(like the Node Name, or Sysop Name).  These can be used
#       to customize your messages.  These may be used in the Message Text
#       here in this file and in those messages read from a file.
#
# Variable     Description
# ^variant     Name of this Citadel Variant such as "Citadel 68K"
# ^version     Major Version Id of Citadel
# ^sysvers     Minor Version Id of Citadel
# ^baseroom    The baseroom of your BBS
# ^sysop       The name of the Sysop
# ^nodetitle   The BBS Node Title
# ^nodename    The BBS Node Name
# ^nodedomain  The Domain the BBS is considered part of
# ^nodeid      The BBS Node Id
# ^mainfloor   The Floor that contains the BaseRoom
# ^curruser    The name of the Current User.
# ^ulprotocols A list of the Protocols usable for uploading
# ^dlprotocols A list of the Protocols usable for downloading
# ^doorlist    A list of the Doors available in the current room
# ^lastuser    The last caller's name
# ^privileges  A list of the privileges you currently have.
# ^callcount   The number of calls this Citadel has recieved.
# ^ia1         Special Integer Argument #1 (see below)
# ^sa1         Special String Argument  #1
# ^ia2         Special Integer Argument #2
# ^sa2         Special String Argument  #2
# ^ia3         Special Integer Argument #3
# ^sa3         Special String Argument  #3
# ^currtime    The current time
# ^currdate    the current date
# ^s           A single space
# ^n           A newline followed by a space
#
# The Special Arguments are pieces of data that are used in some of the
# existing messages.  Currently the 3rd one is not used(but may be).  Most
# of the messages do not use them, but those that do should probably continue
# to use them.  You can remove the special variable from the messages that
# currently do use them, but adding them to a message that does not will get
# you a zero for an interger argument and nothing for a string Fargument.
#
